--TEST--
`<>` is refused as a second spelling of `!=`, at the lexer and by name — `rule:expressions/one-equality-operator`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The comparison itself is legitimate; only the spelling is not, so the
// diagnostic names the operator rather than the operands. The lexer consumes
// `<>` whole and pushes the token it means, which is why this file has one
// error and not a second one about `>`.
int $a = 1;
int $b = 2;
if ($a <> $b) {
    echo "different\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0241]: `<>` is not supported
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
